<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Gaozong of Tang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/628">628</a>–<a href="/years/683">683</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Li Zhi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Gaozong of Tang, Li Zhi, reigned from 649 to 683 and saw Tang power reach its greatest extent with victories over the Western Turks and the conquest of Goguryeo in 668. In 655 he made Wu Zhao his empress, and as he became ill she took over government, later founding her own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Li Zhi was born on 21 July 628, the ninth son of Emperor Taizong and the third son of Empress Zhangsun. He was named Prince of Jin in 631. When Empress Zhangsun died in 636, Taizong was touched by the grief Li Zhi displayed and favoured him from then on. Taizong came to doubt that Li Zhi, who was considered kind but weak in character, was strong enough to be emperor, and secretly discussed with Zhangsun Wuji making another son, Li Ke, crown prince. Zhangsun repeatedly opposed the idea, and Taizong did not carry it out.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Gaozong's first act as emperor was to cancel a second campaign against Goguryeo that Taizong had planned for late 649. In his early reign he greatly respected his uncle Zhangsun Wuji and Chu Suiliang and followed their advice, and the government was well organised. In 650 the general Gao Kan captured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürk</a> leader Ashina Hubo, whom the emperor spared and made a general. In 651 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashina-helu">Ashina Helu</a> broke away from Tang and took over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a>, and the generals sent against him withdrew without engaging him.</p><p>The Tang conquered Baekje, Goguryeo and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a> during the first part of his reign, but Baekje resisters besieged a Tang general at Sabi, and in 662 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/su-dingfang">Su Dingfang</a> withdrew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> after a defeat on the Sasu River and heavy snowstorms. Throughout the 670s much of the territory was lost to the Tibetan Empire, Silla, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> and Balhae. After a series of strokes left him incapacitated, Gaozong delegated all matters of state to Empress Wu from January 665, who was said to be hanging the curtain and listening to politics.</p><p>They were called the two saints, and historians debate whether she exerted undue influence or the two were equal partners. In 674 she took the title Empress of Heaven, and about 675 she submitted twelve suggestions, including that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daodejing">Tao Te Ching</a> be added to the reading of imperial university students; Gaozong praised and adopted them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gaozong died on 27 December 683, and power then passed entirely to Empress Wu as empress dowager and regent. He was interred at the Qian Mausoleum on 25 September 684, and she was buried there after her death in 705. She later became the only empress regnant in Chinese history. In Hui Islamic legend he is called Yung Wei, after the first era name of his reign, and is credited with building the first mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, the Huaisheng Mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Gaozong of Tang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Eastern Turkic Khaganate was destroyed in 630 and Gaochang conquered in 640, and under Taizong's successor <b>Gaozong</b> the defeat of the Western Turkic Khaganate in 657 extended Tang suzerainty across the Tarim Basin and into Transoxiana, bringing Chinese power to its greatest westward extent.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Wu Zhao, a concubine of Emperor Taizong, became empress to <b>Emperor Gaozong</b> in 655 and gradually took over government as he fell ill.</blockquote><a href="/state/wu-zhou">Wu Zhou</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.26,108.94&amp;year=663" aria-label="Xi'an on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7322 922 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Emperor Gaozong of Tang"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7772 1192h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7772 1192h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.26,108.94&amp;year=663">What was here in 663 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
